<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/buddhism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Buddhism is <strong>a diagnosis-and-treatment system for suffering</strong> — unusual among
    traditions for being non-theistic at its core (the existence of gods is beside the point)
    and psychological in method: observe the mind, find craving at the root of distress, train it
    out. Founded by Siddhartha Gautama in the 5th century BCE; now practised across Asia and
    increasingly the West.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the truths, the path, the no-self doctrine, and the schools" widget="equationStep" config={buddhaCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts that travel outside religion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meditation as investigation:</strong> vipassana-style practice became secularised (MBSR, mindfulness research) — the psychology and neuroscience ladders now measure what meditators reported for millennia.</li>
    <li><strong>Impermanence and process philosophy:</strong> anatta parallels Hume's bundle theory and modern cognitive science's distributed self — the philosophy-of-mind lesson connects directly.</li>
    <li><strong>Dependent origination:</strong> everything arises from conditions — an early systems-thinking claim, also the metaphysical basis for the compassion ethic (all beings are conditions of you).</li>
    <li><strong>The historical Buddha is known thin:</strong> born a prince, renounced, awakened at Bodh Gaya, taught ~45 years — details emerge centuries after in texts. Scholars treat the framework as history and the biography as shaped memory.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the Pali Canon (Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta); Rahula, What the Buddha Taught; Gombrich, What the Buddha Thought.</p>
</TopicPage>
